title: Defense Appropriations
new-page: true
is-card: true
tags: govt_affairs, cabinet
on-arrival: defense_appropriations_timer += 6; month_actions += 1
view-if: marcher_in_government = 1 and army_minister_party != "Marcher" and defense_appropriations_timer <= 0
card-image: img/reichstag_2.jpg
#subtitle: The Assembly votes the army its purse.

= Defense Appropriations

The War Ministry is in the hands of the General Staff, not ours — but the army's purse is still voted in the Assembly, and that vote is ours to shape. With the Empire stirring beyond the frontier, the generals press for a larger appropriation. A stronger army would give the Empress pause. A stronger army that does not love the republic, however, is a blade that can be turned inward.

- @appropriate: Vote the army a generous appropriation.
- @restrain: Hold the line on defense spending.
- @root: No change to appropriations.
- @easy_discard
- @cancel_advisor_action

@appropriate
subtitle: -1 budget
choose-if: budget >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: We do not have the budget.
on-arrival: budget -= 1; army_strength += 25; army_militancy += 0.3 if army_militancy < 8; coup_progress += 1 if army_loyalty < 0.3; coup_progress += 1 if army_loyalty < 0.15; dufour_angry -= 5; left_dissent += 3; imperial_fear -= 1 if imperial_fear > 0; economic_growth += 0.1

The Commonwealth Army grows stronger, and the General Staff is gratified — as is President DuFour, who has never trusted a republic that could not defend itself. The Empire's envoys take note. [? if army_loyalty < 0.3 : But the officer corps remains cold to the republic, and a well-armed army that answers to no democrat is its own kind of danger. ?] Our own membership, long suspicious of the generals, is uneasy.

@restrain
choose-if: army_strength > 0
unavailable-subtitle: There is nothing left to restrain.
on-arrival: army_strength -= 10 if army_strength > 0; army_militancy -= 0.1 if army_militancy > 0; coup_progress -= 1 if coup_progress > 0; pacifism += 1; left_dissent -= 2; dufour_angry += 3

We hold the army's purse-strings tight. The generals grumble and the President frowns — a weaker army, he warns, only emboldens the Empress. But the danger of a coup recedes a little, and our own ranks are pleased to see the money go anywhere but the barracks.
